AJ thanks for joining us how you doing  where are you right now I'm in Orlando  Florida where I grew up and where I live  still to this day and dealing with the  ramifications of older MA that just  won't quit how is it there how are the  conditions  well land is good now we took a pretty  good beating on Monday morning Sunday  night late Sunday night about two  o'clock but a lot of trees down a lot of  people still without power just going  around you know it's amazing what this  hurt these hurricanes Harvey and feel  for the people in Texas and then Emma  here in Florida what it's done to people  to bring them together I mean you just  see people out helping each other it's  nice to see how did you prepare for this  well in Orlando we don't get it as bad  we're pretty fortunate we're in the  middle of the state so we don't get the  surge that all the other people get on  the coast and where they get underwater  so we don't have that but for us to  prepare you just try to trim your trees  as much as possible you stock up on  water you stock up on Chef Boyardee  ravioli and SpaghettiOs for the kids and  just hope and pray for the best and it  doesn't get too bad well we appreciate  you joining us a couple of things to  jump into I don't know if you're a big  fan of streaks whether it's good or bad  and what it means for doing that in  September and how that's gonna play out  and what it means in October so look at  the Dodgers look at the Indians will any  of this have an impact on what their  postseason performance will be well the  Dodgers have a history of not winning in  the postseason so anytime you need 11  games in a row in September it's got to  break your psyche a little bit cuz if  you remember two weeks ago everyone was  talking about them being one of the  greatest teams of all time so now all of  a sudden you lose 11 games in their own  people like what happened to the Dodgers  so you can say as a player it's not  going to affect you but the end of the  day it's gonna affect you because you  can't believe they were supposed to have  this team that was built never to lose  and just keep grinding out wins and you  lose 11 games in a row it knocks you  back a little bit but I promise you this  I'd much rather be the Indians win in 20  games in a row than the Dodgers lose in  11 games in a row  but I look at the Indians and it feels  like nationally there's still not a big  story why is that well it's good they're  in Cleveland I mean if you're if the  Cubs would come back and won 20 games in  a row  imagine the headlines they'd be you know  maybe right up there with the 27 Yankees  and and these teams at one so it's just  because they're Cleveland they're a  little bit smaller market and they also  you know this isn't a knock on their  Flyers Kluber is a great a great great  pitcher probably top three or four and  all of baseball but he doesn't command  attention because if you watch him last  night he throws a CG shutout to win 20  in a row he barely cracked a smile it  was like okay great I threw another  shutout I'm probably going to win the  song now and I don't even want to smile  or celebrate it that's what's awesome  about him and then yeah they have Lindor  and they have Ramirez some guys but they  don't command a lot of attention they're  not they're not a group of guys that is  out there just commanding attention  saying hey look at me everyone look at  me they just go about their business and  I think it comes from Francona and he  does a great job getting them to  understand what what baseball is all  about they do it the right way and they  are they are really fun to watch they  need to hire you bring you in they need  to you know somebody who's gonna hate  somebody on the team no no no they're  doing great you know I post baseball is  hard man it's hard sport to play once  you once you're out of it you look back  and you realize how hard it was to do it  every day it's it's hard but we watch  and we'll see a pitch and we'll go how  did you miss that or to change them the  guy swings a mystery like how did you  swing at that and in your mind you're  like man I did that about a thousand  times so I know exactly what they're  thinking and it's a different  perspective but it's fun because being  in being around it still I still get to  go in I still get to see the guys you  see guys are your friends with and guys  you didn't like too much and you're  respectful and they're respectful back  and it's fun to just be a part of it but  it's also it's fun to remember the  struggles and look back and say man I'm  glad I don't have to try to do that  anymore  do you remember a time and I always  wondered why catchers weren't better  hitters because you guys called pitches  you saw more pitches than anybody can  you explain that why catchers aren't  better hitters in general because they  get the crap you get the crap beat out  of them every game that's one reason  okay your hands beat up you get your  head beat up you get your knees beat up  your feet beat up so that's one and then  also just it's hard as a catcher you  don't get to play every day you're not  outfielder I remember watching  outfielders during our games and the  pitch should be coming this was when I  was on the when I wasn't playing and I'd  watch your outfitter and they're working  on their swing in between his doctor you  know chasing a ball around or thinking  about how am I gonna get Giancarlo  Stanton out on the next pitch so I mean  there's it's just such a mental toll  that it takes on you catching it  sometimes hitting gets pushed back to  the back but I mean if you look at Posey  and some of these other guys that are  going now there's some pretty good  hitting catcher is going he's AJ  Pierzynski Fox Sports baseball analyst  joining a Stan Patrick show Red Sox and  the Yankees and stealing signs and now  electronics involved in this how how  often did you guys steal signs or did  you feel like somebody was stealing  signs from you when you played you  always were aware of it I remember we  had a time in Toronto where Rick Hawn  who's a GM now the White Sox was the  assistant GM and he went out there we  had a we were almost a hundred percent  sure that the Blue Jays were stealing  signs and we went out there and I don't  know that the White Sox ever admit this  but we caught a guy with a camera out  there with looking at the Hall of the  catcher sign so we started mixing them  up and one of our pitchers turned around  backwards to the game and the SkyDome  had nobody in it there was a guy in a  white jacket out there and he would  stand up on fast balls and sit down and  off he called every pitch he called  every pitch not looking at the game from  this one guy and so we were like okay we  got to do something about this so we  started mixing it up every inning  changing signs and it was amazing how  much they're hitting and that series  went down after the first game once we  figured that out but I mean it happens  I've never heard of using an Apple watch  to do it it's kind of an ingenious if  you think about it maybe when I was a  red sock if they would have gave that to  me I would had a little better run there  but I don't know I mean they were just  doing it seemed like to relay the  science from second which I get but if  you're if you watch enough so if you  watch enough signs from the videotape  before the game you can pretty much  figure it out if I'm a catcher I can  watch a game you give me you give me  three pitches I can figure out what  signs are using from second base most of  the time would you want to know what  pitch was going to be thrown to you I  only wanted to know if a guy had a pitch  that was really really nasty like if a  guy had a split finger that just I knew  I couldn't hit then I would just not  swing at it because I'm like okay you're  coming to split let's not swing at it if  he throws three in a row because most  the time those pitches are balls and the  problem I ran into when I knew what was  coming a lot of times I'd be like oh  there comes a curveball and they throw  and it bounced in the grass in front of  home plate and I'd swing at it because I  knew a curveball was coming so I just  have to tell myself just don't swing  when's the time that you felt your most  helpless and I know that you're gonna  you know be self-deprecating here but it  was their time when you just stood in  the box and you go this isn't I don't  even know what I'm doing here that you  were that over there was a couple of  pitchers well first of all the one guy  that I always hated facing more than  anybody was a guy you might have heard  of named Mariano Rivera cuz he he had  one pitch he knew it was coming and  being a left-handed hitter I couldn't  hit it he just break that side go up  there and literally and be like alright  he was bad on my breaking day and I'd  ask the guys on my team on your back I  hope you don't like this one because  it's gonna be broken again so he was the  one guy that I was like okay I know  what's coming there's no way for me to  hit this ball hard and keep it fair and  plus every seemed like every time I  faced and the umpire gave him like a  three foot strike zone on each side up  and down and I was like oh man and he  deserved it but I still a little  frustrating but why do we give great  pitchers even more latitude no they're  great they don't need it they've earned  it though they've earned did I  understand I mean remember back when  Greg Maddux and Glavine were at their  prime and their home balls a foot off  the plate and Hobbie  Javy Lopez and any Perez were setting up  a foot off the plate they were all  strikes that was great especially being  a Braves fan it was awesome hey well  good luck with what's going on  poster man there in Orlando always great  to catch up with you we appreciate it AJ  AJ Pierzynski Fox Sports baseball  analyst the Dan Patrick show weekday  mornings on audience

